  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Initial Conditions in Laplace Transforms",
    "lesson_core_move": "Insert the appropriate pre-initial values into one-sided Laplace derivative formulas so initial conditions are carried correctly into $s$-domain equations.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Initial Conditions in Laplace Transforms",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Initial Conditions in Laplace Transforms",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the one-sided (causal) Laplace setup from this module: $\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt$. The key object is the one-sided first-derivative move, which is the only rule that carries initial value data into the $s$-domain.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Define the boundary term directly as the left-hand limit, $x(0^-)=\\lim_{t\\to0^-}x(t)$. This value captures pre-origin behavior, which is what the one-sided derivative integral is sensitive to at the lower limit.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=sX(s)-x(0^-)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the base case with a known pre-initial value: if $x(0^-)=c$, then substitute directly into $\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=sX(s)-x(0^-)$ to get $\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=sX(s)-c$. Keep the subtraction order exactly as written so the sign is correct.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Do not use $x(0^+)$ here. $x(0^+)$ is the first post-origin value and is not the boundary term in the one-sided derivative theorem; substituting it can shift the entire transformed equation by the wrong side-state information.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(0^-)=\\lim_{t\\to 0^-}x(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Next examples will apply this same template in one step: first direct substitution, then zero initial-value checks, then explicit pre- vs post-origin comparison, and finally carrying the corrected term through an $s$-domain equation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Initial Conditions in Laplace Transforms",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Substituting a Given Initial Value into the First-Derivative Property",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}=\\frac{3}{s+1}$ and $x(0^-)=2$, find $\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that both $X(s)$ and the pre-initial value are already provided, so this is a direct substitution step using the one-sided first-derivative identity from the prior tutorial.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=sX(s)-x(0^-)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the identity in symbolic form first, then replace $X(s)$ and $x(0^-)$ with the given values.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=s\\left(\\frac{3}{s+1}\\right)-2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the transformed result is $\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=\\frac{3s}{s+1}-2$. The same subtraction term structure is now visible and reusable for later steps.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Initial Conditions in Laplace Transforms",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Handling Zero Initial Conditions in the First-Derivative Property",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}=\\frac{5}{s^2+9}$ and $x(0^-)=0$, compute $\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the first-derivative rule is known and the pre-initial value is explicitly zero, so this is the boundary-term simplification branch.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=sX(s)-x(0^-)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the given initial condition into the formula: $x(0^-)=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=sX(s)-0 = sX(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now insert the provided transform $X(s)=\\frac{5}{s^2+9}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=s\\left(\\frac{5}{s^2+9}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the transformed derivative is $\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=\\frac{5s}{s^2+9}$, which confirms the zero-initial-condition branch collapses to the core multiplication-by-$s$ form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Initial Conditions in Laplace Transforms",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Distinguishing Pre-Initial Values from Values After the Origin",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Suppose $x(0^-)= -1$ and $x(0^+)=3$ with $\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}=\\frac{2s+1}{s^2+4}$. Find $\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This example is the notation-discrimination case: both a pre-origin and post-origin value are shown, but only the first one belongs in the derivative boundary term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=sX(s)-x(0^-)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the one-sided derivative identity with $x(0^-)$ explicitly; the value $x(0^+)=3$ is a separate post-origin point and is not substituted here.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=s\\left(\\frac{2s+1}{s^2+4}\\right)-(-1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Replace the negative pre-initial value carefully: subtracting $x(0^-)= -1$ adds one.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=\\frac{2s^2+s}{s^2+4}+1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the result is $\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=s\\left(\\frac{2s+1}{s^2+4}\\right)+1$, showing only the pre-initial value drives the additive boundary term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Initial Conditions in Laplace Transforms",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Carrying Initial Conditions into an s-Domain Equation",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Transform $x'(t)+3x(t)=e^{-2t}$ with $x(0^-)=1$ and write the resulting algebraic equation in $s$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the full-equation transfer step: we now carry the initial condition through each transformed term of a differential equation, not just a single transform lookup.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}+3\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}=\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-2t}\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the one-sided derivative rule and the known transform $\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}=X(s)$ with $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-2t}\\}=\\frac{1}{s+2}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "sX(s)-x(0^-)+3X(s)=\\frac{1}{s+2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Insert the pre-initial value explicitly from the cue: $x(0^-)=1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "sX(s)-1+3X(s)=\\frac{1}{s+2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Collect $X(s)$ terms to get the transfer-ready form: $\\left(s+3\\right)X(s)-1=\\frac{1}{s+2}$, or equivalently $X(s)=\\frac{\\frac{1}{s+2}+1}{s+3}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
